  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 AM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nMy favorite thing in the whole world (note my sarcasm) is \ncontinuing this morning, nocturnal monsoonal convection, with \nthe models doing their usual great job with it (more sarcasm). \nThey are gradually weakening though. A couple of showers may \nlinger into the morning but this would be isolated. The trend \ntoday will be for convection chances to trend downward through \nthe day as much drier air punches in from the north and west. \nThere may be enough moisture for a stray shower or thunderstorm \nacross southern Wyoming this afternoon, but the chance is small,\ngenerally less than 1 out of 5. We have some slight POPs in \nthis area to cover for this. However, our attention will turn to\nsomething else. \n\nAnd that is fire weather. The pattern will be the same for the next \nthree days or so. The set up is the same as was discussed yesterday \nWyoming will be roughly the midpoint between an upper level low off \nthe Pacific Northwest coast and riding over the southeastern United \nStates. As a result we will have a fast flow over the area. The 700 \nmillibar winds are fairly strong for this time of year as well, \ngenerally peaking at 35 to 40 knots. Much of the eastern half \nof the area has at least a 1 in 2 chance of wind gusts past 30 \nmph as a result. The limiting factor for fire weather today is \nhumidity. Mean relative humidity among the ensemble members is \ngenerally above 15 percent, although it does get close in \nJohnson County where it drops to around 17 percent. So, for now,\nwe will not issue any fire weather highlights. However, \nelevated to near critical fire weather is likely today for most \nlocations East of the Divide. Temperatures will be rather warm \nas well, generally about 5 degrees above normal with the warmer \nspots like the eastern Bighorn Basin making a run at 90 degrees.\n\nThursday looks like most concerning day for fire weather. This will \nbe the driest day across the area, with humidity generally running \naround 5 percent lower than on Wednesday. Wind will be a bit lighter \nthough, generally be around 5 mph. However, there is still a greater \nthan 1 in 2 chance of wind gusts past 25 mph across much of the \narea, With this, we will issue a fire weather watch across most of \nthe area East of the Divide as well as most of southern Wyoming, \nalthough humidity will be a bit more borderline in this vicinity. \nThe chance of showers looks to be close to zero as well, with the \nonly place with a chance the far northwest where the chance is less \nthan 1 in 5 and largely in the morning. On Friday, we will still \nhave the wind. However, humidity looks somewhat higher, \nespecially across southern Wyoming. We may need additional fire \nweather highlights on this day but will hold off for now since \nthings look more borderline.\n\nAs we head into the weekend, humidity will continue to creep up\nand wind should decrease somewhat on Saturday, and this should \nbe the end of the critical fire weather. The next question is \nabout the next surge of monsoonal moisture and any possible \ninteraction with a tropical cyclone in the western Pacific. \nModels trends are still slower, with most holding off until \nSunday now, and continuing into Monday. Most guidance also takes\nthe tropical cyclone further off of the coast, limiting the \nmoisture somewhat. However, some guidance still gives over an \ninch of precipitable water for Sunday and Monday. Details the \nplacement of the most numerous showers are still uncertain given\nit is still several days out though. Precipitable waters are \nstill expected to climb to over an inch though, so some locally\nheavy rain is still possible. As for temperatures, with the \nridge still expected to retrograde closer to the area, expect \nabove somewhat above normal temperatures to continue into the \nweekend.",
